From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Brad Barnett <127794626+bdbarnett@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 9 Oct 2026 22:00:00 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] dcd_dwc2: disable an endpoint without waiting on flags the
 interrupt handler clears.

Stalling or closing a busy endpoint goes through edpt_disable(). For an
IN endpoint it sets SNAK and polls DIEPINT for NAK effective, then sets
EPDIS and polls DIEPINT for endpoint disabled. handle_ep_irq() writes
back every DIEPINT bit it reads to clear the interrupt, so when another
interrupt of the same endpoint is serviced between the core setting one
of those flags and the poll reading it, the flag is gone and the poll
spins for good. In slave mode a multi-packet IN transfer keeps the
TX FIFO empty interrupt firing, so on an ESP32-S3 a USB disconnect
while the CDC was sending hung the chip for good.

The TX FIFO empty interrupt also kept serving the endpoint being
disabled, pushing packets into a FIFO about to be flushed.

Now edpt_disable():

- masks the endpoint's TX FIFO empty interrupt (DIEPEMPMSK) first. A
  new transfer unmasks it again in edpt_schedule_packets();
- clears NAK effective, sets SNAK and polls for NAK effective, which
  handle_ep_irq() no longer clears (nothing there acts on it);
- waits for EPENA to drop instead of polling endpoint disabled. The core
  clears EPENA once the endpoint is disabled, and the handler never
  writes it. Both polls also end if the endpoint finishes its transfer
  on its own, when no NAK effective or endpoint disabled is due, and
  EPDIS is set only on an endpoint that is still enabled;
- does the same for an OUT endpoint, waiting on EPENA after EPDIS.

Proven on a Waveshare ESP32-S3-Touch-LCD-7 (MicroPython ESP32_GENERIC_S3
LCD_7, native USB on Windows), counting resets and hangs on its UART
console while machine.USBDevice disconnects a busy IN endpoint. Both
builds carry MicroPython's own stall-before-clear reordering
(micropython-pydevices patch 0025); neither holds the USB interrupt off:

  | trial                                          | without | with   |
  |------------------------------------------------|---------|--------|
  | built-in CDC mid-print, active(False)          | 9/40    | 0/89   |
  | built-in CDC mid-print, switch to runtime      | 5/40    | 0/86   |
  | runtime CDC (usb-device-cdc), 16 KB write      | 2/40    | 0/50   |
  | runtime HID mouse, 1 ms reports                | -       | 0/40   |

Every failure without it was a hang, with no REPL on either port.

Based-on: micropython/tinyusb-espressif v0.21.0.1-micropython1 (5c5c660)
---
diff --git a/src/portable/synopsys/dwc2/dcd_dwc2.c b/src/portable/synopsys/dwc2/dcd_dwc2.c
index a866805..740b795 100644
--- a/src/portable/synopsys/dwc2/dcd_dwc2.c
+++ b/src/portable/synopsys/dwc2/dcd_dwc2.c
@@ -315,18 +315,30 @@ static void edpt_disable(uint8_t rhport, uint8_t ep_addr, bool stall) {
   const uint32_t stall_mask = (stall ? EPCTL_STALL : 0);
 
   if (dir == TUSB_DIR_IN) {
+    // Stop the TX FIFO empty interrupt from feeding this endpoint while it is disabled. In slave mode it would go on
+    // pushing packets into a FIFO that is about to be flushed, and fire again for as long as that FIFO is empty.
+    dwc2->diepempmsk &= ~(1u << epnum);
+
     if (!edpt_is_enabled(dep)) {
       dep->diepctl |= DIEPCTL_SNAK | stall_mask;
     } else {
-      // Stop transmitting packets and NAK IN xfers.
+      // Stop transmitting packets and NAK IN xfers. NAK effective is a flag the interrupt handler leaves alone (see
+      // handle_ep_irq), so clear any earlier one first. The endpoint may also finish its transfer on its own meanwhile.
+      dep->diepint = DIEPINT_INEPNE;
       dep->diepctl |= DIEPCTL_SNAK;
-      while ((dep->diepint & DIEPINT_INEPNE) == 0) {}
+      while ((dep->diepint & DIEPINT_INEPNE) == 0 && edpt_is_enabled(dep)) {}
 
-      // Disable the endpoint.
-      dep->diepctl |= DIEPCTL_EPDIS | stall_mask;
-      while ((dep->diepint & DIEPINT_EPDISD_Msk) == 0) {}
+      // Disable the endpoint, and wait on its enable bit, which the core clears once it is disabled and the interrupt
+      // handler never writes. The endpoint disabled flag is no good to poll: the handler clears it whenever another
+      // interrupt of this endpoint is serviced in between, and then the wait never ends.
+      if (edpt_is_enabled(dep)) {
+        dep->diepctl |= DIEPCTL_EPDIS | stall_mask;
+      } else {
+        dep->diepctl |= stall_mask;
+      }
+      while (edpt_is_enabled(dep)) {}
 
-      dep->diepint = DIEPINT_EPDISD;
+      dep->diepint = DIEPINT_EPDISD | DIEPINT_INEPNE;
     }
 
     // Flush the FIFO, and wait until we have confirmed it cleared.
@@ -344,9 +356,14 @@ static void edpt_disable(uint8_t rhport, uint8_t ep_addr, bool stall) {
       dwc2->dctl |= DCTL_SGONAK;
       while ((dwc2->gintsts & GINTSTS_BOUTNAKEFF_Msk) == 0) {}
 
-      // Ditto here disable the endpoint.
-      dep->doepctl |= DOEPCTL_EPDIS | stall_mask;
-      while ((dep->doepint & DOEPINT_EPDISD_Msk) == 0) {}
+      // Ditto here disable the endpoint, waiting on its enable bit as the IN side does: the interrupt handler can
+      // clear the endpoint disabled flag before this reads it, and the endpoint may finish its transfer on its own.
+      if (edpt_is_enabled(dep)) {
+        dep->doepctl |= DOEPCTL_EPDIS | stall_mask;
+      } else {
+        dep->doepctl |= stall_mask;
+      }
+      while (edpt_is_enabled(dep)) {}
 
       dep->doepint = DOEPINT_EPDISD;
 
@@ -1103,7 +1120,11 @@ static void handle_ep_irq(uint8_t rhport, uint8_t dir) {
       } intr;
       intr.value = epout->intr;
 
-      epout->intr = intr.value; // Clear interrupt //-V::2584::{otg_int}
+      // Clear interrupt, except an IN endpoint's NAK effective flag: nothing here acts on it, and edpt_disable() polls
+      // for it with this handler free to run, so clearing it between the core setting it and the poll reading it would
+      // leave that poll spinning. edpt_disable() clears it itself.
+      const uint32_t keep = (dir == TUSB_DIR_IN) ? DIEPINT_INEPNE : 0;
+      epout->intr = intr.value & ~keep; //-V::2584::{otg_int}
 
       if (is_dma) {
         #if CFG_TUD_DWC2_DMA_ENABLE
